---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';

const title = 'Voice Dream Journal for Android | Record Dreams Before They Fade | Somnori';
const description = 'A voice dream journal designed for the middle of the night: capture dreams by speaking, keep the original audio, and review the night in the morning.';
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={description} canonical="/voice-dream-journal" type="software">
  <section class="article-hero">
    <div class="article-shell wide">
      <div class="breadcrumbs"><a href="/">Somnori</a> / Voice Dream Journal</div>
      <p class="eyebrow">Voice dream journal</p>
      <h1>A Voice Dream Journal Built for the Middle of the Night</h1>
      <p class="lead">Speaking is easy. The hard part is capturing a dream before opening a normal app wakes you up enough to lose it. Somnori is built around that exact moment.</p>
      <div class="actions">
        <a class="button primary" href="/hands-free-dream-journal">See hands-free capture</a>
        <a class="button secondary" href="/dream-journal">See the full journal loop</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section-tight">
    <div class="article-shell wide proof-strip">
      <div class="proof-item"><b>Speak naturally</b><span>No need to compose polished text at night.</span></div>
      <div class="proof-item"><b>Keep original audio</b><span>Your voice remains part of the entry.</span></div>
      <div class="proof-item"><b>Review in Morning</b><span>Return to the night's entries after waking.</span></div>
      <div class="proof-item"><b>Android-first</b><span>The night flow is being tested on real devices.</span></div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <article class="article-shell prose section-tight">
    <h2>Why voice works differently at 3AM</h2>
    <p>A traditional dream journal assumes you are awake enough to unlock a phone, find the right screen, type coherent sentences and decide when you are finished. That is a reasonable daytime workflow. It is a poor nighttime one.</p>
    <p>With a voice dream journal, you can record incomplete fragments in the order they return: a place, a face, a feeling, a sentence. The entry does not have to be elegant. It only has to preserve enough of the dream for your morning self.</p>

    <h2>Voice recording is not the unique part</h2>
    <p>Several dream-journal products already support voice. General assistants and voice memo apps can also capture speech. Somnori does not treat the microphone as the differentiator.</p>
    <p>The differentiator we are building around is the <strong>night workflow</strong>: capture with as little screen interaction as possible, keep the original recording, attach it to the night, then continue the process in Morning instead of forcing you to finish everything while half asleep.</p>

    <div class="callout" style="margin:46px 0">
      <p class="eyebrow">Somnori workflow</p>
      <h2 style="margin-bottom:14px">Wake → capture → sleep → review</h2>
      <p class="lead" style="margin-bottom:0">The journal starts before you are fully awake and continues after you are.</p>
    </div>

    <h2>Voice dream journal vs. a normal voice memo</h2>
    <table class="compare-table">
      <thead><tr><th>Need</th><th>Voice memo</th><th>Somnori direction</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Record speech</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Night-specific start flow</td><td>Usually manual</td><td>Designed around the night assistant</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Dream vs. other night entry</td><td>You organize it later</td><td>Entries belong to the night's context</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Morning review</td><td>Open a folder/list</td><td>A dedicated Morning view</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Recurring dream signs</td><td>No dream-specific model</td><td>Part of Somnori's dream-history direction</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h2>What Somnori keeps</h2>
    <p>The current product is designed to preserve the original voice entry rather than replacing it with a generated interpretation. That matters because a transcript can be useful for search, but the original audio contains the hesitations, emphasis and wording you used while the dream was still fresh.</p>
    <p>Morning is where the night becomes readable: review what was recorded, add what came back after waking, and continue building the journal from there.</p>

    <h2>What comes later</h2>
    <p>Somnori's roadmap extends beyond individual recordings toward long-term dream patterns, personal dream signs and a more personalized lucid-dream layer. Those features should only be described as available when they are actually shipped. The site will keep that boundary explicit.</p>

    <h2>Who this is for</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>People who remember dreams briefly and lose them while reaching for a phone.</li>
      <li>People who prefer speaking to typing when they wake during the night.</li>
      <li>People building a long-term dream journal rather than collecting isolated interpretations.</li>
      <li>Lucid dreamers who eventually want recurring signs from their own history to matter.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Start with less friction</h2>
    <p>If your real problem is not voice itself but having to touch and unlock the phone, the more specific page is our <a href="/hands-free-dream-journal">hands-free dream journal</a> guide. If you want the broader category view, see the <a href="/dream-journal">Somnori dream journal</a>.</p>
  </article>

  <section class="section-tight">
    <div class="article-shell wide callout">
      <p class="eyebrow">Android release</p>
      <h2>Capture first. Organize when you are awake.</h2>
      <p class="lead">Somnori is in active physical testing. The public download CTA will replace this release note when the Android build is ready for distribution.</p>
      <div class="actions"><a class="button primary" href="/hands-free-dream-journal">See the no-unlock workflow</a></div>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
